# OPTIONS — Commands, backends, and permissions

File: wiki/Options.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://wiki.openooda.org/#Options.oot

## 1. `ooda` commands

```
ooda check <file.oo> [--json-errors]
ooda build <file.oo> [-o out] [--target c|native|llvm]
ooda run <file.oo>
ooda test <file.oo>
ooda lsp
ooda dump tokens|ast|check <file.oo>
ooda outline <file.oo> [--json]
ooda reflect <file.oo> [symbol] [--json]
ooda fix <file.oo> [--yes]
ooda patch <file.oo> --replace-fn <name> --with <body.oo>
ooda context [path]
ooda digest <file>
ooda health
ooda pkg [--json]
ooda add <name> --vendor <dir>
```

`check` typechecks. It does not run the program.
`build` emits and compiles. Default target is `native` (C + `gcc`).
`run` is check + emit C + `gcc` + exec.
`test` runs `verify` / `assert_eq!` / `assert!`.
`lsp` is a multi-message stdio language server.
`fix` writes only with `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` and a yes (or `--yes`).
`patch` replaces one function body. Absolute paths, `..`, and symlinks are refused.

Also on the CLI: `pm` (package manager), `em`, `migrate`, `pack`, `install`, `context-pack`.
Those are product commands. Treat `pm` as the local vendor path unless you pass a host you trust.

`ooda add` with `--vendor` reads a file tree on disk.
The public vendor files may be served at https://registry.openooda.org.
The CLI does not fetch that host as the default path.

## 2. `oodac` emit options

`oodac` is the compiler. `ooda` calls it.

```
oodac check <file.oo>
oodac check --json-errors <file.oo>
oodac emit-c <file.oo>
oodac emit-llvm <file.oo>
oodac emit-wasm <file.oo>
oodac emit-x86 <file.oo>
oodac --backend llvm emit-llvm <file.oo>
```

`emit-c` is the product emit.
`emit-llvm` writes LLVM IR. Hello and Result `match` have been run through `clang`.
A full `clang` link of the compiler itself is not product. See [Limits](Limits.oot).
`emit-wasm` writes a `\0asm` module with one body per source `fn`.
`emit-x86` writes standalone x86_64 assembly with `_start`. Assemble with `as` and `ld`. AArch64 is not product.

`ooda build --target llvm` and `--emit-llvm` select the LLVM target.
`ooda build --target wasm` is accepted.
`--fuzz` on `build` is refused.
`--release` is only valid with the LLVM target.

## 3. Environment

| Name | What it does |
| :--- | :--- |
| `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR` | Directory `write_file` may write. Unset denies writes. |
| `OODA_FS_READDIR` | Directory confinement for reads. |
| `OODA_TEST` | Set by `ooda test`. You do not set this for `ooda run`. |

`env_get` in a program only sees `OODA_*` and `OO_*` keys. Host secrets stay out.

## 4. Permission tokens

A function that does an effect takes a token argument, for example `&FsReadCap`.
Missing, forged, or zero tokens fail closed.

Common tokens:

| Token | Lets the function |
| :--- | :--- |
| `&FsReadCap` | `read_file`, `path_exists`, `file_size` |
| `&FsWriteCap` | `write_file` (needs `OODA_FS_WRITEDIR`) |
| `&ProcessCap` | `process_exec`, `spawn`, `wait`, `kill` |
| `&NetCap` / `&HttpCap` / `&TcpCap` / `&UdpCap` | network ops |
| `&EnvCap` | `env_get` for `OODA_*` / `OO_*` |
| `&AllocCap` | heap and bump arenas |
| `&ThreadCap` | threads, channels, actor restart |
| `&SysCap` | broad OS (Landlock jail, `setrlimit`, …) |
| `&GpuCap` | CPU/noop launch. Not a device GPU dispatch. |

Prefer the small token. Do not take `&SysCap` when `&FsReadCap` is enough.

On Linux, `&SysCap` can apply a Landlock jail and POSIX `setrlimit`. Empty allowlists and zero quotas fail closed.

## 5. Standard library

Use the small tree that ships with the compiler: `ooda/std`.
That is the product library.

There is a large extra `std/` tree in the org. File count is not a certificate.
Do not import that volume and call it product.

This wiki: https://wiki.openooda.org
